Q1 - Apps team checked and deployed an image on QA env. It was working fine but when done in production, some microservices were not working. How you will deal with the scenario as an SRE and fix it.

Answer - If the application works correctly in QA but fails in Production after deploying the same image, my first assumption is that the image itself may not be the problem. I would compare the Production environment with QA and investigate systematically before taking any action.

Assessing the Impact of this incident - 

a. Need to understand the incident if this is a Sev1 or Sev2

b. Are users seeing errors like 500, 503, timeout, or connection refused?

c. Which microservices are affected.

d. Check relevant deployment, Pods, Services etc.

e. work on restoring the application.

f. After recovery need to work on Root cause Analysis.

Q2 - If you receive a P1 issue, what will be your response as an SRE. give sequential answer

Answer - "When I receive a P1 incident, my first priority is to acknowledge the alert, join the incident bridge, and understand the business impact. I check monitoring dashboards to identify symptoms such as high error rates, latency, or resource exhaustion. Then I verify the Kubernetes/OpenShift cluster health by checking nodes, pods, events, and application logs. I investigate recent deployments or configuration changes and validate dependencies like databases, messaging systems, or external APIs. If the issue is due to a recent deployment or configuration change, I restore service as quickly as possible by rolling back or applying the appropriate fix. Once the service is restored, I validate application health through monitoring and smoke tests, continue monitoring for stability, communicate regular updates to stakeholders, and finally perform a detailed RCA with preventive actions to reduce the likelihood of similar incidents."

This sequence reflects how experienced SREs operate during critical incidents: acknowledge → assess impact → investigate → restore service → validate → communicate → document and improve.

Q3. Tell me what happens when a user tries to access apache server hoster on private subnet in AWS

Answer - The answer depends on how the Apache server in the private subnet is exposed. The most common architecture is:

User (Internet)
      │
      ▼
Route53 (Optional DNS)
      │
      ▼
Internet Gateway (IGW)
      │
      ▼
Public Application Load Balancer (ALB)
      │
      ▼
Target Group
      │
      ▼
Apache Server (EC2) in Private Subnet

"When a user accesses a website hosted on an Apache server in a private subnet, the browser first resolves the domain name using DNS, typically through Route 53, which returns the DNS name of a public Application Load Balancer. The user's HTTPS request reaches the ALB through the Internet Gateway. The ALB terminates SSL if configured, evaluates its listener rules, and forwards the request to a healthy Apache instance in the target group. The Apache EC2 instance resides in a private subnet and has no public IP, so it cannot be accessed directly from the internet. Its security group only allows traffic from the ALB's security group. Apache processes the request and sends the response back through the ALB to the client. If the Apache server itself needs outbound internet access, such as to download updates or call external services, it uses a NAT Gateway in a public subnet, while remaining inaccessible from the internet."

Q4. There is an application running in a service, and other outside the service, but both are on same port. When a user tries to access them will there be port clashing?

Answer - "No, using the same port does not automatically cause a port clash. A network endpoint is identified by the combination of IP address and port. If two applications are running on different hosts, different pods, or behind different Kubernetes Services, they can both use the same port because their IP addresses are different. A port clash only occurs when two processes try to bind to the same IP address and the same port on the same machine. In Kubernetes, each pod has its own IP, so multiple pods can safely listen on the same container port, and Services route traffic to those pods without causing conflicts."

Q5. A deployment with 4 replicas is taking too long to come up, causing a production outage. As an SRE, what actions would you take to minimize the outage?

Answer - As a SRE I would restore service first, investigate systematically, communicate, and implement preventive improvements.

Further details - 

I would check the rollout status, deployment strategy, and pod health to determine why the replicas are not becoming ready. I would review pod logs, events, readiness probes, and check for issues like image pull failures, configuration errors, resource constraints, or dependency failures. If the issue cannot be resolved quickly and production is impacted, I would roll back to the previous stable version or pause the rollout to prevent further disruption. I would also verify cluster health to ensure there are no node or scheduling issues. Once the service is restored, I would validate that all replicas are healthy, monitor the application for stability, and perform a root cause analysis.

Q6. If your application is trying to communicate with other apps and it is not able to then what will happen

Answer - "If my application cannot communicate with another application it depends on, the request may timeout, fail with a connection refused error, or return HTTP errors like 503 or 504. Users may experience slow responses or complete failure of certain features. As an SRE, I would first verify that the dependent service and its pods are running and healthy. Then I'd check the Service and Endpoints to ensure traffic is being routed correctly. Next, I'd review application logs for connection or DNS errors, test connectivity from within the source pod using tools like curl or nslookup, and verify NetworkPolicies, service ports, and readiness probes. Once I identify the root cause, I'd restore connectivity, monitor the application for recovery, and document the incident with preventive actions."

Q7. You have deployed an application for the first time, how you will define SLOs for them

Answer - For a newly deployed application, I would first identify the critical user expectations and define SLIs such as availability, latency, and error rate. Since there is no production history, I would start with reasonable industry-standard SLOs—for example, 99.9% availability, P95 latency under 300–500 ms, and an error rate below 0.1–0.5%. I would ensure the application is instrumented with monitoring and observability tools, collect production data over the first few weeks, and then refine the SLOs based on actual user behavior and business requirements. I would also establish an error budget to balance reliability with the pace of feature delivery.

Q8. How will you make your web app more secure?

Answer - Securing a web application should start with, 'What could go wrong, and how can we prevent it?'. 

a. make sure all communication happens over HTTPS so data is encrypted.
b. make sure users only have access to what they actually need using role-based permissions.
c. make sure secrets like database passwords or API keys are never stored in the code—they should come from a secure secrets manager tool like hashicorp vault
d. enable logging and monitoring so we can quickly detect unusual activity, and keep the servers and dependencies updated with security patches.
e. Finally, I'd include security checks in the CI/CD pipeline, such as vulnerability scans, so we can catch issues before the application reaches production.

Q9. What steps will you take to make your docker image more secure?

Answer - To make my docker image more secure - 

a. try to use trusted and lightweight base image, because the fewer packages we have, the smaller the attack surface.
b. avoid running the application as the root user. Instead, I create a non-root user inside the container so that even if someone manages to compromise it, they won't have full privileges.
c. Never store secrets in the image.
d. Remove unnecessary packages using multi-stage builds.
e. Scan the image for vulnerabilities (e.g., Trivy).
f. Keep the image and dependencies updated.

Q10. What is timeout, retry, cascading effect for application resilience, and how it can be solved.

Answer - "Application resilience is about making sure an application continues to work even when some components fail. Three common concepts are timeouts, retries, and the cascading effect."

Easy way to remember
Timeout → "Don't wait forever."
Retry → "Try again, but don't overwhelm the service."
Cascading Failure → "One failing service shouldn't bring down the entire application."
Solution → Timeout + Controlled Retry + Circuit Breaker + Monitoring + Autoscaling + Graceful Degradation.

Q11. Explain SRE Principles.

Answer - From my understanding, SRE (Site Reliability Engineering) is about balancing reliability with the speed of delivering new features. The idea is to build systems that are reliable, automate repetitive work, and use data to make decisions instead of guesswork.

The main SRE principles are:

1. Define Reliability with SLOs - The first principle is to define what reliability actually means. Instead of saying 'the application should never go down,' we define measurable targets using SLOs, like 99.9% availability or 95% of requests completing within 300 milliseconds.

2. Manage Error Budgets - An error budget is the amount of acceptable failure based on the SLO. For example, if the SLO is 99.9% availability, a small amount of downtime is acceptable.

3. Automate Repetitive Tasks - SREs try to eliminate manual, repetitive work by automating it. Things like deployments, infrastructure provisioning, backups, monitoring setup, and incident response should be automated as much as possible.

4. Monitoring and Observability - You can't improve what you can't measure. That's why monitoring and observability are key principles. We collect metrics, logs, and traces so we can quickly detect issues, identify the root cause, and understand how the application is performing

5. Incident Response and Learning - Incidents will happen, so the goal is to respond quickly, restore the service, and then learn from what happened. After major incidents, teams conduct a blameless postmortem to identify improvements without focusing on individual mistakes

6. Reduce Toil - Toil refers to repetitive manual work that doesn't add long-term value, like repeatedly restarting services or manually deploying applications. SRE aims to reduce this by automating those tasks.

7. Design for Resilience - Applications should be able to handle failures gracefully. That means using timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, load balancing, redundancy, and autoscaling so that a failure in one component doesn't bring down the entire system.

Q12. What is SLI, SLO and SLA?

Answer - 
SLI(Service Level Indicator) = What we measure.
SLO(Service Level Objective) = The target we want to achieve.
SLA(Service Level Agreement) = The promise we make to the customer.

Simple Example (Easy to Remember)

Imagine you're running an online shopping website.

Term	Example
SLI	Measure how many checkout requests succeed and how fast they complete.
SLO	99.9% of checkouts should succeed, and 95% should complete within 2 seconds.
SLA	Promise customers 99.9% uptime per month. If it's lower, they receive service credits.

Q13. What is latency and throughput?

Answer - Latency and throughput are two important performance metrics in computing, networking, and distributed systems.
Latency is the time it takes for a single request or operation to complete.

Measured in units of time (milliseconds, seconds, etc.).
Lower latency is usually better.

Throughput is the amount of work completed in a given period of time.

Measured in requests per second (RPS), transactions per second (TPS), MB/s, etc.
Higher throughput is usually better.

